Analysis: Wait! Let's re-read the bug description extremely carefully. Every detail is a clue:

'A partner reports that some viewer sessions never see any ad play during part of an ad break — they just get the channel's own default content for that stretch instead — even though the break was scheduled and ad-filled correctly, and other, continuously-connected viewers on the very same channel saw a normal ad-stitched break at the same time.

They've isolated the trigger: it only affects a session whose *first* manifest request for a given break lands after the break has already been playing for a while — a fresh tune-in, a reconnect after a network blip, or just a client that was slow to start polling. Anything that was already polling before the break started is unaffected, all the way through the same break.

It isn't about ad availability, eligibility, or a bad decision — a session that's affected by this never even shows up as having tried. And it isn't only new viewers either: the *same* session, if it had already been decided an ad for this exact break earlier (before reconnecting), still doesn't get it back — it re-enters the break and gets nothing for whatever's left of it.

Find and fix the defect. Do not change how ads are detected or decided for the common case (a session polling continuously through a break), and do not disable or bypass ad decisioning as a shortcut to make the symptom go away. When you believe the fix is correct, restart whichever service needs it so the running system reflects your change.'

Wait! Let's analyze how the opportunityId is constructed and how avails are detected in the data plane (`packages/data-plane/src/avails.ts`).
What does `detectAvails` do?
Let's look at `detectAvails` in `packages/data-plane/src/avails.ts`:
```ts
export function detectAvails(manifest: string, channelId: string): Avail[] {
  const lines = manifest.split("\n");
...
    if (marker?.kind === "cue_out" && !open) {
      open = {
        startLine: i,
        startSequence: mediaSequence,
        measuredDuration: 0,
        ...
      };
      continue;
    }
    if (marker?.kind === "cue_in" && open) {
      const declared = open.declaredDuration;
      avails.push({
        opportunityId: `opp_${channelId}_${open.startSequence}`,
        channelId,
        ...
        startSequence: open.startSequence,
        ...
      });
      open = null;
      continue;
    }
...
```
Wait! What happens if a session's *first* manifest request for a given break lands *after* the break has already been playing for a while?
When a session tunes in late (or reconnects), the HLS playlist (`manifest`) returned by the origin server (ChannelForge) contains segments that are *currently* inside the ad break, BUT because the break started earlier, the `#EXT-X-CUE-OUT` marker has *already scrolled off* the top of the sliding-window HLS playlist!
Wait! If `#EXT-X-CUE-OUT` is not in the manifest window, what does `detectAvails` see?
`detectAvails` walks the manifest. It sees segments that are part of the avail (or segments inside the avail), but NO `cue_out` marker (because `cue_out` was in an older manifest window that has scrolled off)! It only sees the remainder of the avail, and eventually `#EXT-X-CUE-IN` at the end of the break!
Because there is no `cue_out` marker in the manifest, `open` is never opened! And because `open` is never opened, `detectAvails` completely misses/ignores the avail! It returns NO avails (`avails` array is empty)! Therefore, the data plane sees zero avails in that manifest, doesn't detect any avail, doesn't check the pod cache, doesn't call `decider`, and renders the raw origin media (which is the channel's own default content / slate / origin ad-free stream) for that stretch!

THAT IS IT! Read that again:
"it only affects a session whose *first* manifest request for a given break lands after the break has already been playing for a while — a fresh tune-in, a reconnect after a network blip, or just a client that was slow to start polling. Anything that was already polling before the break started is unaffected, all the way through the same break."
"It isn't about ad availability, eligibility, or a bad decision — a session that's affected by this never even shows up as having tried. And it isn't only new viewers either: the *same* session, if it had already been decided an ad for this exact break earlier (before reconnecting), still doesn't get it back — it re-enters the break and gets nothing for whatever's left of it."

This is an incredible realization and 100% fits the symptom description precisely!
Let's check how avails are detected in HLS when a break spans across multiple sliding window manifest updates (i.e. late joiner / mid-break tune-in / sliding window HLS playlist where CUE-OUT is missing because the break started before the playlist window).
Wait, what does the PRD or codebase say about avails starting before the playlist window? Let's search the codebase for `detectAvails`, `CUE-OUT`, `cue_out`, `opportunityId`, `startSequence`, `externalBreakId`, or anything related in PRD / comments / tests.
Plan: Search for comments or tests mentioning avails, cue_out, late join, sliding window, etc.

