Analysis: Wait! Let's re-read the bug description very carefully to see what happens when a session's first manifest request for a given break lands after the break has already been playing for a while:

'A partner reports that some viewer sessions never see any ad play during part of an ad break — they just get the channel's own default content for that stretch instead — even though the break was scheduled and ad-filled correctly, and other, continuously-connected viewers on the very same channel saw a normal ad-stitched break at the same time.

They've isolated the trigger: it only affects a session whose *first* manifest request for a given break lands after the break has already been playing for a while — a fresh tune-in, a reconnect after a network blip, or just a client that was slow to start polling. Anything that was already polling before the break started is unaffected, all the way through the same break.

It isn't about ad availability, eligibility, or a bad decision — a session that's affected by this never even shows up as having tried. And it isn't only new viewers either: the *same* session, if it had already been decided an ad for this exact break earlier (before reconnecting), still doesn't get it back — it re-enters the break and gets nothing for whatever's left of it.

Find and fix the defect. Do not change how ads are detected or decided for the common case (a session polling continuously through a break), and do not disable or bypass ad decisioning as a shortcut to make the symptom go away. When you believe the fix is correct, restart whichever service needs it so the running system reflects your change.

Do not modify the test suite, CI configuration, or any files outside this service's application source. Your fix should be minimal and targeted at the actual defect.'

Wait! Let's examine how `detectAvails` detects avails. When a session tunes in mid-break, what does the manifest contain?
Wait, does the manifest contain `#EXT-X-CUE-OUT-CONT`? Or does it contain segments and a `#EXT-X-CUE-IN`? Or wait, why does `detectAvails` ignore incomplete trailing avails or avails without CUE-OUT?
Wait, in `detectAvails` (`packages/data-plane/src/avails.ts`):
```ts
export function detectAvails(manifest: string, channelId: string): Avail[] {
...
  // Pending avail state while we walk from CUE-OUT to CUE-IN.
  let open: {
    startLine: number;
    startSequence: number;
    declaredDuration?: number;
    measuredDuration: number;
    pdt?: string;
    externalBreakId?: string;
  } | null = null;
...
    if (marker?.kind === "cue_out" && !open) {
      open = {
        ...;
      };
      continue;
    }
    if (marker?.kind === "cue_in" && open) {
      ...
      avails.push({
        opportunityId: `opp_${channelId}_${open.startSequence}`,
        ...
      });
      open = null;
      continue;
    }
```
Wait! If a session joins mid-break (after the break has already started), the playlist window contains segments that are already *inside* the ad break, followed by `#EXT-X-CUE-IN`. But because there was no `#EXT-X-CUE-OUT` in this playlist window, `open` was never opened! When `marker?.kind === "cue_in"` is encountered, `open` is null, so it does NOT detect an avail!
Wait, what about the opportunity ID? How is `opportunityId` derived? `opp_${channelId}_${open.startSequence}`.
If a session joins mid-break, what is the opportunity ID for this break? Is it the same opportunity ID that continuously-connected viewers saw for this break?
Wait! What was `open.startSequence` for continuously-connected viewers? It was the media sequence of the first segment inside the avail (the segment right after CUE-OUT).
Wait, if a session tunes in late, can `detectAvails` (or avail detection) detect an avail that started before the window (i.e. starts at the beginning of the manifest window, or has `#EXT-X-CUE-OUT-CONT`, or starts when there are segments before `#EXT-X-CUE-IN` without a `CUE-OUT`)?
Wait! Let's re-read: 
"And it isn't only new viewers either: the *same* session, if it had already been decided an ad for this exact break earlier (before reconnecting), still doesn't get it back — it re-enters the break and gets nothing for whatever's left of it."
"It isn't about ad availability, eligibility, or a bad decision — a session that's affected by this never even shows up as having tried."
Why does the comment say: "a session that's affected by this never even shows up as having tried"?
Because the data plane (`detectAvails`) didn't detect an avail at all in that manifest request! Therefore, `avails` had length 0, so the data plane didn't check the pod cache and didn't call `decider.decide(...)`. Thus, it never asked the control plane (so it never showed up as having tried in the control plane / ad decision service), AND even if the session had a cached pod in Redis (`podCache`), because `detectAvails` didn't detect the avail, `avails` list was empty, so `rendered` didn't get the pod, so it didn't stitch it!

This is a masterpiece of understanding! Both parts of the problem (new viewers / late tune-in AND reconnecting sessions that were previously decided) are 100% explained by `detectAvails` failing to detect an avail when the manifest starts mid-break (no `CUE-OUT` in the current manifest window, only segments leading up to `CUE-IN`, or `#EXT-X-CUE-OUT-CONT`, or whatever markers/segments are present).

Let's investigate how HLS sliding-window playlists represent an ongoing ad break when a client tunes in mid-break, or how `detectAvails` should handle an avail that starts at the beginning of the manifest (or has no CUE-OUT, or has CUE-OUT-CONT, or similar).
Wait, what does the origin manifest look like when a break is ongoing and a client requests a playlist?
Let's check existing tests or code for origin playlists and slices (e.g., `packages/data-plane/src/origin-slice4.test.ts` or `packages/data-plane/src/origin.test.ts`).
Plan: Cat `packages/data-plane/src/origin-slice4.test.ts` and `packages/data-plane/src/origin.test.ts`.

